      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69917,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 543,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 505,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69918,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Prostate</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69919,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "<p>Most patients present with urinary obstructive symptoms and increased urinary frequency. As many as 72% have had a prior diagnosis of prostate adenocarcinoma, for which they were often treated by hormone and/or radiation therapy; 29% of patients present without a history of adenocarcinoma {15105655}. As many as 20% of patients present with metastatic disease {21139962}. Serum&nbsp;PSA levels can be elevated or normal.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-04-07T16:27:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69920,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenosquamous carcinoma represents 0.003% of all prostate cancers {32141804}. It is less commonly seen than pure squamous cell carcinoma of the prostate. Median reported ages vary from 67&nbsp;to 74 years {21139962; 32141804}. The tumour arises either de&nbsp;novo or after radiation and/or hormone therapy {15105655}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69921,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most patients have a history of conventional prostatic adenocarcinoma for which they were treated by radiation and/or hormone therapy {15105655}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69922,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The molecular background is comparable to that of conventional acinar adenocarcinoma, with <em>TMPRSS2</em>::<em>ERG</em> fusions, <em>PTEN</em> deletions, and <em>SPOP</em> mutations {32639612; 31882336}. In reported <em>ERG</em>-rearranged cases, immunohistochemical staining was strongly positive in the glandular component but weak to negative in the squamous component {32639612}. One adenosquamous carcinoma with <em>FAM131A</em>::<em>BRAF</em> fusion has been reported {32639612}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69923,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not well established</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69924,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "<p>Adenosquamous carcinoma is composed of both glandular (acinar) and squamous components. Most cases show varying proportions of the squamous component admixed with the glandular component. In most cases, the acinar component is grade group&nbsp;2 or higher. The squamous component can range from 5% to 95%, and it can show a wide range of differentiation as well as mild to severe cytological atypia {15105655}. Nuclei in the squamous component are larger and more pleomorphic than in the glandular component. The glandular or squamous component can be distinct or show transitions from one to the other. Transitions of both components are frequently observed. A third component, sarcomatous, is observed in 20&ndash;57% of cases {15105655; 32639612}. The glandular component expresses PSA or other prostate markers such as PAP; the squamous component is positive for basal cell markers while being mostly negative for PSA {15105655}. There are only scant data on NKX3-1 {31882336}.</p>\r\n<p>The most important differential diagnosis is urothelial carcinoma with divergent differentiation, from which adenosquamous carcinoma can be distinguished by the morphology and immunohistochemical profile of the non-squamous component as well as by consideration of the clinical history and radiological appearance.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-04-14T14:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69925,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 512,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 551,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69926,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 551,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Molecular investigation (for <em>TMPRSS2</em>::<em>ERG</em> fusions, <em>PTEN</em> deletions, and <em>SPOP</em> mutations) may be helpful in selected cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 515,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 554,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69927,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 554,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> presence of both malignant glandular and squamous components.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 513,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 552,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69928,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 552,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) or Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification is used.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 514,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 553,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 69929,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 105,
        "headingId": 553,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenosquamous carcinoma is a rare and aggressive disease, with 20&ndash;31% of men presenting with metastasis, and with 5-year cancer-specific survival rates of 30% {21139962; 32141804}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-10T19:39:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]